window.docContents[103].resolve({"/Environment/#Cvc___lean----High-Level-API-for-CVC5--Environment--___":{"contents":"This API abstracts away the term manager completely and enforces manager-consistency statically using a type-class called Ω. Virtually all types in this library have an implicit Ω argument that can be thought of as representing a specific term manager. On the sort type for instance:\n\n#check Srt\n\n\nCvc.Srt [Ω] : Type\n\n\nΩ is an empty Prop type-class and will be erased at runtime; note that you cannot build an instance yourself as the constructor is private.\n\nArgument of the Env monad representing a specific term manager.A Type, deliberately not a Prop. Any two proofs of a Prop are definitionally equal, so a\nProp-valued Ω made a sort of one scope the same type as a sort of any other, and nested\nEnv.runs could mix, or leak into each other, their sorts and terms. Two instances of this class\nbound by two runs are distinct variables, so @Srt ω and @Srt ω' are distinct types.Its one field is a type, and that is load-bearing twice over. A structure with no field, or a\nfield of type Unit, would be distinct in name only: structure eta makes all its values equal,\nas proof irrelevance did. And a field holding a type is erased by the compiler, so an instance is\nrepresented as a constant scalar — no allocation, no reference counting — and is dropped entirely\nfrom the body of every function that does not use it.@[nospecialize] because, as data, an instance would otherwise be a specialization candidate like\nany other: the compiler would copy bodies from this module — private helpers included, which other\nmodules may not reference — into its callers, to specialize them on an argument every scope gives\nthe same constant value.\n\nΩ is tied to cvc.lean's environment monad Env: Env α, or more precisely @Env inst α where (inst : Ω), corresponds to code that uses a specific term manager to produce a value of type α.\nCvc5's term manager handles term/sort perfect sharingAlso known as hash-consing: building the same term twice produces the same (reference to the same) term, meaning physical (reference) equality is structural equality..\n\nThe first problem Ω and Env solve is that manipulating a value v tied to manager m after m has been freed is unsafe as freeing m frees v, so v should only live as long as m is alive. So most features in this library produce an Env _ output.\n\nSecond, as mentioned above, values tied to different managers should never interact as that would cause a runtime error. By encoding a specific term manager through an Ω-value, cvc.lean can type-check that sorts/terms/solvers/etc. are compatible by making sure they agree on their Ω-value.\n\nFor example, the function building an array-sort makes sure the index and element sorts agree on their manager, and keeps track of the manager associated to the resulting sort by having all of them agree on their Ω.\n\nCreates the sort of arrays from idx to elm, e.g. idx.arrayTo elm.\n\n","context":"Cvc.lean -- High-Level API for CVC5\u0009Environment","header":"2.1. Ω","id":"/Environment/#Cvc___lean----High-Level-API-for-CVC5--Environment--___"}});